00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.080
Both __new__ and __init__ are part of the
process of constructing an object.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.390
Both of them get called when you try to create
an instance of a class.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.670
In this case, we see that the new method gets
called with Class A, and then the arguments

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.369
and keyword arguments, whereas the __init__
method gets passed an actual instance of the

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:13.759
[class] and then the same arguments and keyword
arguments.

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:18.630
Here is approximately what happens when you
actually execute a line of code like this.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.890
First, the __new__ method is called with the
given arguments and keyword arguments.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:28.979
Then, if the returned object has the correct
type, then it will go ahead and call the __init__

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:29.979
method.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:33.490
So this really points out what the difference
is between __new__ and __init__.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:39.969
__new__ is responsible for creating and returning
the actual object, whereas __init__ is responsible

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:43.639
for initializing it, setting default values
and things like that.

00:01:43.639 --> 00:01:46.990
We can further confirm this by looking at
the return types.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:51.520
_new__ is actually supposed to return something,
an object, whereas __init__ doesn't return

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.520
anything.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.020
It just initializes values.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.289
This explains why you don't return anything
from __init__.

00:01:57.289 --> 00:01:59.819
Its __new__
s job to actually make the new object.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:05.710
It's a class method, so it takes in the class
that it's supposed to return an instance of,

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.560
and then it needs to return an instance of
that class.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.580
Now notice here because of this isinstance
check, the __init__ method will only be called

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.610
if it does actually return an object of the
given type.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.050
If I return the wrong kind of object from
__new__, then I'll just be left with an uninitialized

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.400
object and __init__ will never be called.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.340
We can see this happen by commenting out the
return line so it returns None.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.680
Now we see that __new__ was called, but __init__
was never called, and the value of x is None.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.760
That can be really annoying, so always remember
to return something from __new__.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:36.760
That's its job.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.209
I'd say that most Python programmers learn
about __init__ fairly early.

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:44.180
Pretty much as soon as they learn about classes,
you learn about __init__ and how to make your

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.610
own classes that have their own attributes.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.130
But learning about __new__ is something that
usually doesn't come up until much, much later,

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:52.349
sometimes years even.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:57.290
So, when would you ever need to actually change
how the object is created rather than just

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:58.500
how it's initialized?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:05.310
__new__ was added into Python, primarily to
allow programmers to subclass built-in immutable

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.319
types.

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:09.270
Suppose that I want to make an uppercase tuple
type.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:13.450
It's going to be the exact same thing as a
tuple, except whenever you create one, it

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.390
always uppercases its arguments.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.239
You might be tempted to try to accomplish
this using the __init__ function.

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:21.500
You take in an iterable as its argument.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.880
You're going to loop through those things,
change the ith thing and the tuple to the

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.299
uppercase version of that argument.

00:03:28.299 --> 00:03:31.439
But when we run the code, we see that that
doesn't work.

00:03:31.439 --> 00:03:34.530
By the time that __init__ is called, it's
too late.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:38.310
The object already exists and it cannot be
changed.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:43.730
The only possible way to get around this is
to intercept and modify the arguments before

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.750
the object is created.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.659
This can be accomplished using __new__.

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:50.950
Here we take in the same iterable that we
would have taken into __init__ and then we

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.090
modify the arguments.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:55.999
We create a generator here that's going to
be a new iterable that uppercases all the

00:03:55.999 --> 00:04:00.859
strings that it sees, and then we go ahead
and pass that new iterable to the tuple class.

00:04:00.859 --> 00:04:03.689
Then we end up with an actual uppercase tuple.

00:04:03.689 --> 00:04:08.420
Again, this is only possible because we're
modifying the arguments before this immutable

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.140
thing is actually created.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.370
Now, when we run the example, it works.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:17.549
We have lowercase "hi" and "there", and when
it's printed out, we have uppercase "HI" and

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:18.549
"THERE."

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:23.190
You might be wondering in a case like this,
why wouldn't I just create a class that contains

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.650
a tuple instead of inheriting from tuple?

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.660
And that's a very valid point.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.210
The reason, at least for a case like this,
would be performance.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.009
You could certainly make a proxy object that
uppercases the arguments and then forwards

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:38.020
all of its method calls to the underlying
tuple.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.690
However, doing that is going to significantly
impact the performance.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:47.639
Tuple is a built-in python object, primarily
written in C. Because it's written in C that

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:50.940
makes it much faster than any Python code
that you could write.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.890
So if you wrote a wrapper around it, that
was proxying all the calls, that wrapper would

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.540
be written in Python, and it would take a
significant performance hit.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.370
You would also be in a similar situation for
any type written as a C-extension.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.780
Here's another interesting way that __new__
can be used.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.639
Creational design patterns.

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:12.460
Here is an implementation of what's called
the singleton design pattern.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.690
The purpose of a singleton is that there's
only supposed to be one of them.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:20.910
You might think about it like a global configuration
object that everything is supposed to share,

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.330
no matter how many times you try to create
one.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.780
You're always supposed to get back the same
instance.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.400
Since you can only ever have one instance
of a singleton, that prevents everyone from

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.500
getting out of sync.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.750
They just all have a reference to the same
object.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:37.389
So if you have something like a global config,
you could change an option in one place and

00:05:37.389 --> 00:05:41.500
then it would immediately be available to
all of the other places that had instances

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.550
of that object.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.350
Personally, I don't think you should ever
use a singleton.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:49.010
It has a lot of the same problems that just
keeping everything in a global variable would

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:50.010
have.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.639
And for that reason, I can't recommend it.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:54.440
But, it does showcase an interesting use case.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:58.960
When you try to create the singleton, if there's
no instance that's already set, then it actually

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.160
creates one.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.169
However, if the instance has already been
set, then we just return the existing instance.

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:06.840
Notice an important detail here.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.180
__new__ is supposed to return an instance
of the class.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.770
However, I never said it had to return a *new*
instance.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.879
It's perfectly fine to return something that
already exists.

00:06:16.879 --> 00:06:22.039
We can see this example code to check that
there is actually only one instance of the

00:06:22.039 --> 00:06:23.039
singleton.

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:24.539
So here, we create the first one.

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:28.740
Then we create another one, and then we check
that they are literally the same object, and

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.360
indeed we see that X is Y equals true.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.080
So it was literally the same object that was
returned from the two calls to the constructor.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.129
Here's another example that's like the singleton
example on steroids.

00:06:39.129 --> 00:06:43.460
Imagine you have an object that is incredibly
expensive to initialize.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.770
Maybe initialization requires something like
going out to a database to read the results

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.669
or reading it from a file.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:53.150
If that's the case, you definitely don't want
to create a new instance if you already have

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:54.380
one in memory.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:58.449
The loaded variable here keeps track of which
clients have been loaded from the database.

00:06:58.449 --> 00:07:00.430
Whenever we're asked to create a new instance.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:04.620
First, we check if there's already an existing
one that has the same Client ID.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.250
If there's already an existing one, we just
return that.

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:12.150
If not, then we go ahead and create the new
client, mark them as loaded and then do whatever.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.610
go out to the database to initialize them.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.120
This _init_from_file function, think about
as actually going out to the database and

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.290
doing all the hard work.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:24.270
I didn't actually implement any of that stuff,
but this is similar to how some big frameworks

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:25.270
work.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:28.830
When we actually use the client class, the
first time that we construct a client with

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:34.580
ID 0, it goes out and reads the client from
the file or database or whatever.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:37.479
But the second time it just returns the existing
one.

00:07:37.479 --> 00:07:41.560
But if we try to construct a client with a
different client ID, then it does go out to

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.610
the database again and read that one.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:48.550
Doing it this way minimizes the number of
times that we have to make those really expensive

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:50.860
calls, like going out to the database.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:55.039
One could argue that a lot of these examples
are probably better suited for a factory pattern

00:07:55.039 --> 00:07:59.530
rather than overwriting __new__, but this
video is about learning how you can use __new__.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:01.900
Ok, Here's another really fun one.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:06.360
Imagine that we want an encrypted file class
that's able to read encrypted files.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.189
Basically, you just pass it the key to the
file and then tell it what kind of encryption

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:13.800
is used and where the file is located.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.979
Of course, we don't want to have one mega
class that just has every different possible

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:20.600
kind of encryption and methods for it inside.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:25.110
Instead, we'll have different classes for
different kinds of encryption.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:29.159
For a plain text file, we just open the file
and return its contents.

00:08:29.159 --> 00:08:36.380
But for a ROT 13 file, we would read the file
and then decode it as ROT 13 and return that.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:41.130
If we had a file that was encrypted with a
one time pad that we're supposed to XOR with,

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:47.110
then we read the file as bytes, xor the bytes
with the key to the file and then decode and

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.110
return that.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:52.430
Now all of the logic for how to read each
kind of encrypted file is delegated to the

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:53.899
class that's in charge of that.

00:08:53.899 --> 00:08:58.380
But, I don't want to do something like hard
code plaintext file here because this thing

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:01.339
might be an argument of the function that's
not known ahead of time.

00:09:01.339 --> 00:09:05.490
I want to just pass the string that has all
the information to encrypted file and get

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:07.500
back the right kind of class.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.540
Here's how we accomplish that.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:14.709
First off, we'll use this registry dictionary
to map from prefixes like "rot13" to classes

00:09:14.709 --> 00:09:17.500
that actually implement the functionality
for those.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.440
We populate the registry by using this __init_subclass__
hook.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:25.820
This function is called whenever a class subclasses
EncryptedFile.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:31.300
Rot13Text subclasses EncryptedFile and has
a prefix of "rot13".

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.230
When this line defining the class is executed,
it calls the __init_subclass__ function of

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:37.810
the EncryptedFile class.

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:43.440
It'll pass in the subclass, the Rot13Text
subclass, along with the prefix that it had.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.070
Then we just store the prefix and class in
the registry.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:51.160
Now, in the __new__ method of EncryptedFile,
we just parse out the prefix from the path

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:55.970
and then look that prefix up in the registry
that'll tell us which subclass that we should

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:56.970
use.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:00.230
We then create a new instance of that subclass
and return it.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:04.889
So if the path starts with "file" colon, we
return a plain text object.

00:10:04.889 --> 00:10:10.240
But if it starts with "rot13", then we return
a Rot13Text object.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:14.250
Here we have three text files that when you
print them out, you see this: the first one

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:18.660
"hello world", the second one jumbled up mess,
the third one another jumbled up mess.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:23.970
But, once I add in the correct prefixes "rot13"
and one time pad ("otp") with the correct

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:27.050
key, then I see something else.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:30.811
After decrypting, we see that the first two
said "hello world" and the last one said "subscribe

00:10:30.811 --> 00:10:33.970
to mcoding".

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:39.220
The last very common use case for overriding
__new__ is metaclasses.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:43.190
Metaclasses very often want to or need to
override the __new__ method.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.940
I don't want to go into the details about
metaclasses too much.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.360
I'm going to have a whole new video about
them next week.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.290
Be sure to stay tuned.

